      "text": "First, we need to factorize the number of factors that are multiples of 12 into the product of their prime factors. Let these factors be represented by p and q. Denote n = 96 = 2* 48 (p, q = 2, prime)\n\nCalculating primes q, we have:\npq = 96 = (2 * 2*2 * 2*2*3*1)\npq = 2 (3 prime), 4\nn = p* q  &  p = 96 / n\n\nComparing the two options, it can be concluded that there is only one such factor whose prime factors are p and q.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{1}.\n\n**Note:** In the given solution, it should be $pq = 96 = (2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 3) × 1$. Incorrectly, it is written as $pq = 96 = 2 (3 prime)$. \n\n**Another Note:** The reason why there is only one factor is due to the fact that the only factors of the number are 2 and 4. Those are the only factors of 96 which are multiples of 12 because all other double factor could be been $96 * \\frac{12}{2} = 576$, which is not a factor. So you can only take $96 * 12 = \\boxed{12}$. So the answer is $\\boxed{96*12}$.",
